This one promises some interesting digital forensics challenges, so let's dive in!
We start with another zip file with password "btlo", so let's extract that first and then look at the questions.

What is the camera model?
Seems we have to start with some metadata. Let's right click the image and go to Properties. From there, go to the Image tab, and we see:
Camera Model: Canon EOS 550D

When was the picture taken?
Same tab, there's a Date Taken line:
2021:11:02 13:20:23

What does the comment on the first image says? 
Ah, something we can't find from the Properties tab!
I remember using exiftool in my SANS courses, which can read and write metadata that maybe isn't shown in the Properties. Syntax is:
exiftool -f [file]
Where the -f switch forces the command to output all tags. I had to look this one up, as just running "exiftool pfile]" does not give us any comments.
After running, we see this line:
Comment: relying on altered metadata to catch me?

Where could the criminal be?
Well, the handy dandy exiftool also gives us this information with the previous command:
GPS Latitude                    : 32 deg 40' 3.87" S
GPS Longitude                   : 279 deg 29' 31.87" W
But if we try to look this up, we get an error saying this is invalid information.
Next idea; let's try a reverse image search. Sometimes we have to backtrack to a simpler solution rather than going right for GPS coordinates.
Using Google's reverse image search, it is revealed that they are likely in Kathmandu.
